From a778c5786aca10afbf9feca669f617fa8b9ee327 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EcoWoods Build <build@ecowoods.ca>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 16:47:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(authority): provenance and grading papers, five species
 dossiers, sourced citations
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WHAT THIS IS FOR

This site already out-documents its market on how a floor is installed. It said
nothing about where the wood comes from or how it is graded — the two questions
underneath every specification decision, and the two where the public record is
almost entirely unsourced SEO content. This closes both, from primary documents
only.

TWO NEW TECHNICAL PAPERS

  /papers/where-toronto-hardwood-comes-from   "Provenance", 11 sections
  /papers/hardwood-grading-standards-nhla-nwfa "Grade", 9 sections

Every figure in them was read from a document published by the organisation
named beside it, on 2026-08-27: Ontario's forest inventory and silvicultural
guides, the NHLA 2023 rulebook, the NWFA/NOFMA April 2018 flooring standard, the
USDA Forest Products Laboratory Wood Handbook, AHEC's species guide, the EPA's
formaldehyde limits, and the manufacturers' own disclosures.

The longest section in Provenance is `not-published` — fifteen things we looked
for and could not source, listed so nobody has to take the rest on trust. It
includes the claim that red oak historically dominates Ontario housing stock,
which is repeated everywhere in this industry and which we could not attribute to
any authority. We do not publish it.

FIVE SPECIES DOSSIERS, NOT FIVE CASE STUDIES

  /guides/red-oak-flooring-toronto
  /guides/hard-maple-flooring-toronto
  /guides/white-ash-flooring-toronto
  /guides/hickory-flooring-toronto
  /guides/black-walnut-flooring-toronto

The five case studies already on this site are real jobs with measured pre-work
readings. Five invented ones would have read identically and passed every guard
in this repository, because the guards test consistency and not truth — and they
would have withdrawn the credit the true ones earned. These are case studies of
the MATERIAL instead: published hardness, Ontario range and commercial status,
standing inventory, growth against harvest, and the grades each species is
actually sold in.

The headline finding: white ash is the only one of the six species used in this
market being cut faster than it grows — 3.3 million m³ annual growth against 6.9
million m³ harvested. That is emerald ash borer salvage, not demand, and any
honest ash specification has to start there.

PAPERS NOW CARRY THEIR SOURCES

`Paper.references` records organisation, exact title, the organisation's own URL
and the date a human opened it. Rendered as a numbered register at the foot of
each paper, and emitted as schema.org `citation` — the field an answer engine
reads to decide whether a document is grounded or merely asserted.

THE PDF IS NOW DERIVED FROM THE PAGE

scripts/gen-paper-tex.mjs renders every paper in the manifest to LaTeX. The
hand-written .tex beside a manifest-driven page was two documents that agreed
until the day they did not. Now there is one manuscript. Run: pnpm papers:tex

IMAGES: WIRED AHEAD OF THE ART, DELIBERATELY

Seventeen figure ids are already named in the two page templates.
<Illustration> returns null for an id the manifest does not carry, so they render
nothing today — no placeholder, no broken icon, no reserved space. The manifest
entries cannot ship first: apps/web/public is not served on this deployment, so
every slot needs a static import, and the import is generated from files on disk.
`status: 'pending'` is unreachable here for a slot with no file, whatever
lib/images.ts says.

scripts/add-provenance-slots.mjs closes that gap in one command once the art
lands. It parses docs/illustrations/PROVENANCE_IMAGE_BRIEF.md — the brief the
illustrator was actually given — so the manifest cannot describe a picture
different from the one that was commissioned, and it reads each file's real
dimensions rather than trusting the brief's canvas.

ALSO

  · 2 new plotted figures (Ontario growing stock; the NHLA yield ladder), both
    verified against numbers that appear verbatim in their cited section
  · 12 new glossary terms, every one citing a paper section
  · 5 new standards register entries. FPL-GTR-190 is marked revision-open on
    purpose: a 2021 edition exists, and extracting hardness from it returns
    column-slipped values that do not convert between units. The note says so.
  · the five dossiers sit UNDER the white-oak canonical in the topic map rather
    than beside it. Five new clusters claiming "white oak vs red oak" is split
    intent, which search engines resolve by ranking neither page.

All 36 guards green.

